which marked the final defeat of the Song."}, {"sourceSlug": "emperor-bing-of-song", "sourceName": "Emperor Bing of Song", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "guangzhou", "targetName": "Guangzhou, China", "context": "Emperor Bing's final moments and the decisive Battle of Yamen occurred in the coastal waters near Guangzhou in March 1279."}, {"sourceSlug": "lu-xiufu", "sourceName": "Lu Xiufu", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "emperor-bing-of-song", "targetName": "Emperor Bing of Song", "context": "As Chancellor, Lu Xiufu served as Emperor Bing's primary protector and regent, attempting to govern the Song court-in-exile from 1278 to 1279."}], "places": [{"name": "Guangzhou, China", "role": "Site of final defeat"}, {"name": "Fuzhou, China", "role": "Initial enthronement location"}, {"name": "Yaishan, China", "role": "Battle location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Emperor Bing of Song", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "emperor-bing-of-song"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7503", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhao_Bing"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Emperor Bing's brief and tragic reign marked the definitive end of the Song dynasty, leading to the complete Mongol conquest of China and the establishment of the Yuan Dynasty. His death symbolized the final collapse of a major Chinese imperial power after over 300 years.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-22T08:11:22Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32561504202", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Zhao Bing, also known as Emperor Bing of Song or Bing, Emperor of Song (宋帝昺), was the 18th and last emperor of the Song dynasty of China, who ruled as a minor between 6 and 7 years of age.", "newValue": "Zhao Bing, the eighteenth and final emperor of the Song dynasty of China, reigned for a brief but tragically significant period from 1278 to 1279. Ascending the throne at the tender age of six or seven, his rule was a desperate, symbolic last stand against the overwhelming Mongol forces of Kublai Khan, representing the definitive end of over three centuries of Song imperial power and cultural brilliance. His short life and reign are inextricably linked to the final, cataclysmic moments of a once-mighty Chinese empire.

Proclaimed emperor at Gangzhou (modern Fuzhou) in 1278 following the death of his elder brother Emperor Duanzong, Zhao Bing's court was a government-in-exile, a fleet of ships constantly fleeing the relentless Mongol pursuit. Loyal officials, most notably Chancellor Lu Xiufu, attempted to preserve the dynastic line and maintain a semblance of state authority. Despite their efforts, the child emperor remained a powerless figurehead, his presence primarily serving as a rallying point for a doomed resistance. The final, decisive confrontation occurred at the Battle of Yamen in March 1279, off the coast of Guangdong.

The catastrophic defeat at Yamen signaled the complete collapse of the Southern Song dynasty, leaving China entirely under Mongol rule for the first time in its history. Emperor Bing's death, along with thousands of loyal Song officials, soldiers, and civilians who chose suicide over capture by the Yuan forces, cemented the end of an era. The Song, a dynasty renowned for its technological innovation, economic prosperity, and sophisticated culture, was extinguished, ushering in the foreign-led Yuan Dynasty across all of China."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-22T08:11:22Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32561504202", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1272-02-19, Hangzhou"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1272-02-19, Hangzhou"}], "newValue": ["The relentless military pressure and territorial gains of the Mongol Yuan dynasty directly led to the flight and eventual collapse of the Southern Song imperial court.", "The death of Emperor Duanzong in 1278, while fleeing Mongol forces, necessitated the enthronement of his younger brother, Zhao Bing, to maintain dynastic legitimacy.", "The unwavering loyalty of key Song officials, such as Lu Xiufu, who refused to surrender and sought to preserve the Song imperial line despite overwhelming odds."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-22T08:11:22Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32561504202", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1279-03-26"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1279-03-26"}], "newValue": ["The death of Emperor Bing and the Battle of Yamen marked the absolute and undisputed end of the Song dynasty, conclu